#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_C \\left(x^2 + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc3a36829c9780c34e13f253d607c3849bfd3ef1399a77a79a3dd3d30ef7140.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553804 :question-generator/id #uuid "b90ae8e8-7088-501b-8712-c1249f136dc7" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133893.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5c27e6-6d15-5543-baca-509a958d1428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369253} {:answer/id #uuid "81b4c97c-6573-5711-9cd1-f51a4a264e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369254} {:answer/id #uuid "4b80e13c-c214-5a76-9bff-a55badcb706d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369255} {:answer/id #uuid "938debfc-b447-5b7c-a524-0c933d4da736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369256} {:answer/id #uuid "977e7ec8-5c46-57e8-b421-0c2f023be513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977e7ec8-5c46-57e8-b421-0c2f023be513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369257} :answer-field/id #uuid "29184995-008b-5dbe-af80-c01d22170858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdf2c9bf-2367-5618-ad0e-1a35e799a6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124120" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(0+t(1-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & =1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099c51d1-91fb-55ee-987f-bff891e71b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256866} {:answer/id #uuid "36c1045e-ca0b-598a-af18-e80132339c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257262} {:answer/id #uuid "86d6990c-bef3-51f4-9954-5afedefe853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257965} {:answer/id #uuid "889b3465-0076-578d-8931-4a15986da352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257979} {:answer/id #uuid "fd053697-39b2-5e77-9cf0-6997020d6328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d6990c-bef3-51f4-9954-5afedefe853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257965} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94ee333-488f-593d-8e2c-fc868b0959b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb08f302-8cb5-5978-acbb-0bba1cddced3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124123" :question/problem "![](images/84/8417d2d62e9213ec3f92108495107fc982dc399e52cca8982e01516457f5692a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40dc00cd-4c24-526f-b0be-714180e4f0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257359} {:answer/id #uuid "5febbd5f-4713-5fff-8f7f-17efac6589cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257622} {:answer/id #uuid "7696a3d2-8e7a-5c22-b23e-1b2752090aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257825} {:answer/id #uuid "ad788cdb-65a4-5dfe-965b-6ccf2a1c8f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258298} {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ebaf3f-afef-5801-a181-f3c0188294e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0853e6c9-d856-5aec-b1f2-e5d65484f5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124167" :question/problem "![](images/55/559a6b6cb717c10e60b77da402e310fc5895c814655b4ca1748eba294d712a32.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper half of the circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57205941-d594-5f13-9a16-fe0cd389d946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257534} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb566f1-93cb-5af8-a57a-1b33ba0d8412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257733} {:answer/id #uuid "74b195e5-6b2c-5425-b2b5-b8110e1a0731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257803} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c06057-adaf-5508-83fe-4fb2c2b5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478} {:answer/id #uuid "e3249ef7-a202-5faa-95b6-80450e4666cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c06057-adaf-5508-83fe-4fb2c2b5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478} :answer-field/id #uuid "124db33d-09bf-50c0-a6e0-28e8a683c661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "105c34ef-d15b-56de-a1a8-f1b42bfb4fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-124173" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbd3fdd67ac209544717fc7d4b33831c7cdfd9f6839a7c1410f43ffd385d63ff.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(0,-1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021c19b6-a87f-59bb-986b-654c56b918a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256802} {:answer/id #uuid "54fdfd50-de07-5cfb-b6d6-270b66947185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257507} {:answer/id #uuid "6abe279c-1db9-56ef-99fe-6e272def1150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} {:answer/id #uuid "debdbff0-5139-5a2f-ac8e-84956824c0b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258722} {:answer/id #uuid "f66ec332-aeec-5f7f-8bb0-93630c7f3749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abe279c-1db9-56ef-99fe-6e272def1150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} :answer-field/id #uuid "4358961a-328e-53e0-ad60-06b7463ba396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd888ac9-9398-5cd7-abd9-7da37aaa7d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-124175" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\sqrt{x-1}+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ceacbe-c767-5fbf-a196-9dc0a6e3ef27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256975} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2a09aa-6780-57b9-84e1-0804ea1c0959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257023} {:answer/id #uuid "37384247-4488-580e-ab77-fc38b0f10247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257266} {:answer/id #uuid "3999c92c-0ae4-51a9-9073-6aeee6da3e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257290} {:answer/id #uuid "7eedef54-bfcb-5d25-a867-579313c708bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eedef54-bfcb-5d25-a867-579313c708bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea48476d-dc43-5cf6-bf5c-5a8d7d48b4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f008788-4322-589f-b4f5-5bd6b468b9b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124177" :question/problem "![](images/27/272b0afabca579f65cbaa7949fd1be70427ff0fd860eb953207bbc8eda7b6e9c.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{3x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-left quarter of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb77b4b-9f0a-5ae7-b350-e9c8ee7ac7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257019} {:answer/id #uuid "50f9dd65-239e-59ad-8a3d-93b2debc6b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257473} {:answer/id #uuid "6b988f18-d213-513d-9832-3e63e11d42ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257732} {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} {:answer/id #uuid "f63874ba-42bf-5ec0-a115-516773880a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4362a76-2e86-5ca7-8502-9a0cc1964b8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdf631e5-2841-5a67-a8ff-d0eebc8b7e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-124212" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfa95100566fce9ab113b00ae53d1a76e8977b68aa4bac0e9b6892a9930b70d4.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the lower half of the circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f7b9b1-6205-5e9a-abd6-d1dfcd93b57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256979} {:answer/id #uuid "614bd43e-4103-5afd-b258-686e690c6a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257637} {:answer/id #uuid "ac79318b-520f-5618-92c3-a94976c39773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258284} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e76e62-2425-5791-b451-5fd5063d747e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5e0345-52da-5c07-878f-0a4c916fd920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd5e0345-52da-5c07-878f-0a4c916fd920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e95fb8c-bf91-5786-bbd2-5640b44aefed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1879edf5-1344-5830-a3f2-7e9af982bae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124217" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb4c4828626255e26e298a0cdc202b37852df9d39bcfe15e0e67d350d90f774a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(2-2)),\\,(0+t(2-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})+{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (2t)}{2})+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4+\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2[4-(-1)+1] \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57321071-ed92-5969-b645-38b2e65f944b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257537} {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} {:answer/id #uuid "6c038848-0fc0-55d3-b62d-414a628f5e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(e+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257735} {:answer/id #uuid "707e588a-fbf2-5a85-9ad2-4cf1abd166f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257768} {:answer/id #uuid "de648ba3-07ad-5cb0-a4a0-5469d13b5ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae7cf10-da4c-5368-b5cf-081f5c11280f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11aa222d-e9ea-5ecd-ab97-032f5281bcab" :question/math-academy-id "q-124273" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce947fb0-2af2-5578-bc88-f97fd8bc5195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369274} {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4e0df-ee12-54ca-90c1-55fd453aaffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369276} {:answer/id #uuid "17c11c14-5bd8-5dfc-9bc8-39959c08d204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369277} {:answer/id #uuid "9230ca12-f5f1-5580-9077-a7bbc7def856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f91420-1dd1-57a3-bbc3-7cd1a732535b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb41f199-2f33-5f68-9a53-dffe3c93ab84" :question/math-academy-id "q-124268" :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b329fb-ed63-5c9c-ba4f-1ae88547c789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411696} {:answer/id #uuid "3565a038-62ce-5c59-8e95-c3505e80c675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411705} {:answer/id #uuid "46436b71-8e85-5a39-bf27-5ed0f753e896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411714} {:answer/id #uuid "c32ac31a-d68d-5bbe-8d37-51261f4658fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411784} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd4223-88ec-5c0a-a0b3-1dc095b07709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd4223-88ec-5c0a-a0b3-1dc095b07709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411816} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed96f7f-49a8-554d-a91d-a5525992b220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58242fe6-4f94-5872-a2af-27a25e52256a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124278" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\sqrt{3y})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(0,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(0-2)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{3y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2-2t)+\\sqrt{3(3t)} \\\\  & =2-2t+3\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3{t}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{[2t-{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3[(2(1)-(1)^{2}+2(1)^{3/2})-(0)] \\\\  & =3(3) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21702398-6680-552d-923f-027dff95a35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527383} {:answer/id #uuid "6029d8a7-a6ae-5959-9ee4-f8e3ebd8e072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527489} {:answer/id #uuid "78ae96ca-4942-5362-807b-c0b65d2496c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527533} {:answer/id #uuid "c94fc212-35ff-5e38-9f57-12d4f4959a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527711} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8f272e-677d-5489-96d8-1e29db88e990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6029d8a7-a6ae-5959-9ee4-f8e3ebd8e072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527489} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9c47c5-4e96-58fa-ac91-5791dc11f30c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ada3d128-ac86-51c2-88c8-3e672e4cad88" :question/math-academy-id "q-124172" :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+2t)^{2}-2(2t) \\\\  & =1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09332eae-5b2a-514b-8867-2093558d05b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553770} {:answer/id #uuid "420c6f0f-9dce-576e-9b0d-e95c7499ccb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\left(\\frac{11}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553778} {:answer/id #uuid "776051fe-e8fa-5d70-877e-b7f12d71dc6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553782} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa03e7-07e0-5b72-9c4a-d1951c6e832e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553790} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8388c4-b4b0-5c49-a3e6-c0618fd0f8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "776051fe-e8fa-5d70-877e-b7f12d71dc6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553782} :answer-field/id #uuid "93450f91-e2e7-5d78-85f6-4f4af1e8b902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0da10636-70da-5542-b982-fb7b4f87a3a4" :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}9\\left({y}^{2}-2x\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9\\left({y}^{2}-2x\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\left((1+2t)^{2}-2(2t)\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(4{t}^{2}+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0)\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0752d09c-5f6a-5f53-b2ee-4d14923c79a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553769} {:answer/id #uuid "618f68da-24b3-5f0a-805d-fbaacb6ec826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(\\frac{13}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553780} {:answer/id #uuid "83f08410-f8d4-5fcb-88cc-cce40721c333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553783} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab18849-82d2-5380-bfc1-cc92cf4de014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553787} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5790a3-b0de-5077-a139-ed86ea6ba02f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "618f68da-24b3-5f0a-805d-fbaacb6ec826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(\\frac{13}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553780} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a51f296-5463-5808-9339-98064322cbb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "108f6b56-f476-502d-ac1b-82f7c7bee892" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}3\\left(\\sqrt{x-1}+y\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3\\left(\\sqrt{x-1}+y\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\left(\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left(\\sqrt{t}-t+2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left({[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left((\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0)\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{13}{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbd8d3d-bf94-5248-baf7-04662b6aadb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left({e}^{2}+1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553775} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a00b88-cc53-5b01-b917-ddbe6814de6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(\\frac{3}{2}(e+1)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553793} {:answer/id #uuid "e4284b1e-3a30-52a4-94c0-1caf9580e943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(\\frac{e}{2}-1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553797} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e12e4a-0957-5b8e-85c2-01f0b3e26b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553801} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc5da25-166a-5b6b-820b-9a27ca4a5b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(e-1)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8e12e4a-0957-5b8e-85c2-01f0b3e26b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553801} :answer-field/id #uuid "efbebf4f-659b-5cc4-bb41-66562e488f37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b42b22b-83d6-590d-b354-39580363db2b" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}7\\left(x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left(x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((3\\operatorname{cos}t){e}^{1-\\operatorname{sin}^{2}t}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\left(-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right)& =7\\left(-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28779ab3-b956-50e6-818b-e6f98c82ef13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(9\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553773} {:answer/id #uuid "39e080af-d60a-5141-9d7b-170da955f54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553776} {:answer/id #uuid "842f3d35-4a65-5fbb-a1f8-17b9bb34c7fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(-2\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553784} {:answer/id #uuid "aa31a872-ecbe-5369-ab2a-5b1f9b98b2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(-6\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553788} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a561cd-3c03-570c-916f-6173a02ece12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(5\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39e080af-d60a-5141-9d7b-170da955f54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553776} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee29c494-9956-584f-b89c-04ed9aab541e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d3f41fe-2ff6-5192-9b2b-6d5b3c48b78f" :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}7\\left({x}^{2}y\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left({x}^{2}y\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\left(4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)& =7\\left(4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-(-1)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "688703f1-baed-5882-b609-dee232bef6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(3\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553781} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7df652-c5bc-5ca9-bc05-48812058f7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553785} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ecac19-efd7-59bf-a437-69523cb8f1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(9\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553792} {:answer/id #uuid "d6390df0-7054-59fa-85ef-6b7ce29f20c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(8\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553795} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9c1463-2e33-5db8-a497-c62cd8d39bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(12\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1ecac19-efd7-59bf-a437-69523cb8f1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\left(9\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553792} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae189165-2609-5ae1-b76b-109d1c7e5259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c44f9146-1b6c-5fb1-ad95-4b025e298a3e" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}7\\left(x+\\sqrt{3y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(0,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(0-2)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left(x+\\sqrt{3y}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((2-2t)+\\sqrt{3(3t)}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3{t}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3{[2t-{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3[(2(1)-(1)^{2}+2(1)^{3/2})-(0)]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3(3)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(9\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}]}
